WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.760
- [Narrator] Glaciers have been shaping

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.320
our world for millions of years.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.850
But as climate change warms the planet,

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.050
glaciers are disappearing, not
only altering the landscapes

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.660
they leave behind, but
changing our oceans, weather,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.733
and life on earth as we know it.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.903
A glacier is a huge mass of ice
that moves slowly over land.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.710
Glaciers can be classified
into two general groups

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.960
alpine glaciers and ice sheets.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.370
Alpine glaciers form on mountainsides

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.990
and move downward through valleys.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:40.830
Sometimes, they create
valleys by pushing dirt, soil,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.853
and other materials out of their way.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.300
These glaciers are found

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.890
on every continent except Australia.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.620
Ice sheets, unlike alpine glaciers,

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.570
are not limited to mountainous areas.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.980
They form broad domes and spread out

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.353
from their centers in all directions.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.610
As ice sheets spread, they
cover everything around them

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.430
with a thick blanket of
ice, including valleys,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.520
plains, and even entire mountains.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.870
The largest ice sheets are
called continental glaciers.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.610
They cover vast area
including most of Antarctica

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.273
and the island of Greenland.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.530
Glaciers can form over years

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.263
when more snow piles up and melts.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.820
Soon after falling, the
snow begins to become denser

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.860
and more tightly packed.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.800
When new snow falls and buries
the previous years snow,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.200
the bottom layer becomes
even more compressed.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.470
The dense, grainy ice that has survived

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.203
one year's melt cycle is called firn.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.520
When the ice grows thick
enough, the firn grains fuse

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.550
into a huge mass called glacial ice.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.430
The glacier may begin to
move under its own weight,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.113
through a process called
compression melting.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.690
As they move, glaciers erode or wear away

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.693
the land beneath and around them.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.700
When glaciers began their latest retreat

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.650
less than 20,000 years
ago, they left behind

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.400
many landscape features,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.093
such as lakes, valleys, and mountains.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.653
Glaciers provide people
with many useful resources.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.140
Glacial till provides fertile
soil for growing crops.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.350
And, deposits of sand and gravel are used

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.660
to make concrete and asphalt.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.010
Many rivers are fed by the
melting ice of glaciers.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.150
The most important resource provided

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.233
by glaciers is freshwater.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.990
Earth's average temperature
has been increasing

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.253
dramatically for more than a century.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.310
Glaciers can act as
indicators of global warming

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.170
and climate change in several ways.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.120
Melting ice sheets contribute
to rising sea levels.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.940
As ice sheets in Antarctica
and Greenland melt,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.180
they raise sea levels, adding fresh water

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.650
to the ocean every day.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.500
The loss of glacial ice
also reduces the amount

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.733
of fresh water available for
plants and animals on land.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.210
Large additions of fresh water change

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.683
the ocean ecosystem,
as well ocean currents.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.470
Additionally, less salt
in the ocean could disturb

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.860
the gulf stream, drastically changing

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.683
the weather on land as well.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.640
Since glaciers are so
sensitive to climate change,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.670
the increased speed of glacier melt

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.583
is an early warning system
for the rest of the planet.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.930
And if global warming goes
unchecked, many, if not all,

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.260
alpine glaciers could
disappear completely.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.760
(soft entertaining music)

